# Blog posts: each 1,000-1,200 words of article content
BLOG_POSTS = [
{
"slug": "why-word-counts-matter",
"title": "Why Word Counts Matter: A Practical Guide for Writers and Editors",
"desc": "Word limits are not bureaucracy. A practical guide to why word counts matter, how to hit them, and how to edit toward a target without gutting your work.",
"date": "2025-06-01",
"blocks": [
("p", "Every writer eventually meets a number. An essay that must be 2,000 words. A blog post that cannot exceed 1,200. A meta description with room for 155 characters. A freelance contract that pays per word. Word counts are often treated as bureaucracy to be tolerated, but they exist for good reasons, and learning to work with them, rather than resenting them, is one of the most practical skills in writing."),
("p", "The principles below apply whether you are a student, a content marketer, a technical writer or a novelist negotiating with an editor."),
("h2", "Why limits exist in the first place"),
("p", "Most word limits encode a real constraint on the reader's side. Assignment lengths exist because a course must compare work fairly, and a fixed range is the only fair yardstick. Publication limits exist because attention is finite: a 3,000-word newsletter competes with everything else in the reader's inbox, and the editor knows the drop-off point from experience. Platform limits exist because of hard technical boundaries, a title tag truncated by a search engine, a message truncated by a screen."),
("p", "There is a second, less obvious reason: length forces editing, and editing forces quality. A writer with unlimited space pads. A writer with a 1,000-word budget must decide what actually matters. The limit is not the enemy of the writing; it is often the reason the writing gets good. Many editors will tell you that cutting a draft from 1,600 words to 1,100 is where the piece is actually written."),
("h2", "The three numbers that matter more than the total"),
("h3", "Words are the unit of substance"),
("p", "Word count measures substance: how much material the reader must absorb. When a brief says 1,000 words, it is making a claim about depth, about how much explanation, evidence and nuance the topic deserves. Under-shooting usually means the topic was not actually covered; overshooting usually means something in the piece is doing no work."),
("h3", "Characters are the unit of space"),
("p", "Character counts measure physical space, and they govern the web. Search engines truncate titles around 60 characters and descriptions around 160. Social platforms enforce their own limits. A headline that is brilliant at 74 characters simply will not display in full, so the last fourteen characters of polish are invisible. Watching characters, not words, is what fits copy into fixed slots."),
("h3", "Reading time is the unit of attention"),
("p", "Reading time converts length into the currency readers actually spend: minutes. At a typical adult silent-reading speed of 200 to 250 words per minute, a 1,000-word article is a four-to-five-minute commitment. Framing length this way changes decisions: a 2,500-word piece is not merely long, it is a ten-minute ask, and the content must earn ten minutes."),
("h2", "How to hit a target without gutting the work"),
("p", "The amateur approach to a 1,000-word target is to write 700 words and pad, or write 1,400 and butcher. Both produce worse writing than aiming correctly from the start. Professionals work differently, and their habits are learnable."),
("h3", "Plan to the budget"),
("p", "Before drafting, allocate the budget across sections. A 1,000-word article might spend 120 words on the introduction, 150 on each of four main sections, 120 on a practical example, and the rest on a conclusion. This allocation, written down before drafting, is the difference between hitting the target and discovering at the end that one section consumed half the budget."),
("h3", "Draft slightly over, then cut with rules"),
("p", "Draft to about ten percent over the target, then cut with rules rather than mood. Cut any sentence that only restates the previous one. Cut adverbs that duplicate the verb's meaning. Cut examples that illustrate the same point as another example. Cut the second-best quotation and keep the best. Each rule removes words without removing information, and together they routinely find ten to fifteen percent of a draft that can go."),
("h3", "Expand with substance, never with air"),
("p", "When a draft is under target, the temptation is to stretch sentences. Resist it. The honest ways to add length all add value: add an example that makes an abstract point concrete, add a step that a beginner would need, add a caveat that a careful reader would raise, add a short FAQ answering the question the piece inevitably provokes. Each of these earns its words. Air does not."),
("h2", "A workflow that keeps counts honest"),
("p", "Checking the word count after finishing a draft is like weighing luggage after packing: accurate, but too late to be convenient. Live counting changes the process. When the counter is visible while writing, section budgets enforce themselves, and the final count lands near the target without a crisis edit."),
("p", "For long documents, count section by section, because a 9,000-word draft with one 4,000-word section has a structure problem that a total will not reveal."),
("h2", "When to break the rules"),
("p", "Every guideline has exceptions, and word counts are no different. Some topics genuinely need 3,000 words; some announcements genuinely need 80. The skill is knowing when a limit is a real constraint, external and non-negotiable, versus a convention, internal and adjustable. External limits are met, always. Internal conventions are negotiated with evidence: if the draft is 1,350 words and every section is doing work, the right move is often to raise the limit, not to cut."),
("p", "What is never acceptable is padding: repetition dressed as emphasis, throat-clearing introductions, FAQs added as decoration. Readers and editors detect filler instantly, and filler costs more credibility than the extra words could ever buy."),
("h2", "The takeaway"),
("p", "Word counts are not the enemy of good writing; unedited writing is. Treat the limit as part of the assignment, budget sections before drafting, watch counts live rather than at the end, and expand only with substance. Writers who internalise this stop experiencing limits as obstacles and start using them as the editing force that makes their work tighter, clearer and more readable."),
("faq", [
("How do I count words accurately?", "Use a tool that counts words the way word processors do: any run of visible characters separated by spaces is one word. A live counter that updates as you type is more useful than a final check, because it lets you steer section by section."),
("Do word counts include headings and FAQs?", "Conventions vary by publisher. As a rule, count the body content the reader will read: paragraphs, lists and FAQ answers. Navigation, buttons and interface text are never counted."),
("What is a good blog post length?", "Long enough to cover the topic with substance, which for most practical guides lands between 1,000 and 1,500 words. Shorter posts suit announcements; longer ones suit deep technical material."),
("How can I cut 200 words quickly?", "Hunt restatements first, then adverbs duplicating verbs, then duplicate examples. These three passes almost always find ten percent of a draft without touching substance."),
("Is reading time a real metric?", "Yes, with caveats. It is an estimate based on average silent-reading speed, but it is the most honest way to communicate a piece's cost in attention, which is what readers and editors actually care about."),
]),
],
},
{
"slug": "strong-passwords-guide",
"title": "Strong Passwords: A Practical Guide to Better Password Security",
"desc": "Most account break-ins exploit weak and reused passwords, not clever hacks. A practical guide to password strength, entropy, managers and the habits that matter.",
"date": "2025-06-05",
"blocks": [
("p", "When an account gets broken into, the cause is usually not a dramatic hack. It is a password: short, reused, guessable, or all three. Studies of large breaches consistently show that the majority of compromised accounts fell to credential stuffing, where attackers take username and password pairs leaked from one service and try them everywhere else, betting correctly that people reuse passwords."),
("p", "The good news is that the defence is unglamorous and effective: long, random, unique passwords, stored in a password manager, with two-factor authentication layered on top. This guide explains what actually makes a password strong, why the advice works, and how to put it into practice without making your digital life harder."),
("h2", "What makes a password strong"),
("h3", "Length beats everything"),
("p", "Password strength against guessing is measured in entropy, counted in bits, and each bit doubles the number of guesses an attacker needs. The fastest way to add bits is to add characters. An eight-character password using every symbol on the keyboard has about 52 bits of entropy; a sixteen-character password of just lowercase letters has about 75. Length wins because every extra character multiplies the search space, while complexity only widens the pool once."),
("h3", "Randomness beats cleverness"),
("p", "Humans are terrible random generators. Substituting a for an at, adding a year to the end, capitalising the first letter: these tricks are so universal that cracking tools try them first, by the millions. A password's real entropy comes from unpredictability, not from looking complicated. That is why generated random passwords, or long random passphrases, outperform any human-invented pattern of the same length."),
("h3", "Uniqueness contains the damage"),
("p", "A perfect password reused on ten sites is ten chances to leak. When any one of those services is breached, the attackers hold the key to the other nine. Uniqueness does not make any single password stronger; it makes a breach stop at the border of the one compromised service instead of cascading through your life."),
("h2", "The password manager changes everything"),
("p", "The objection to unique random passwords has always been memory: nobody can remember fifty random strings. Password managers solve this completely. You remember one strong master passphrase; the manager generates, stores and fills unique passwords for everything else. Auto-fill also protects against phishing, because a manager will not fill your bank password into a site that merely looks like your bank."),
("p", "Choosing one comes down to picking a reputable option, cloud-based or local, enabling a strong master passphrase of four or more unrelated words, and keeping a written backup of that passphrase somewhere physically secure. The transition weekend looks like this: install the manager, generate new passwords for your five most critical accounts, then upgrade others opportunistically as you log in over the following weeks."),
("h2", "Two-factor authentication closes the remaining gap"),
("p", "A password is something an attacker can steal: from a breached database, from malware, from a phishing page. Two-factor authentication adds a second, independent proof, usually a time-based code from an app or a hardware key, so a stolen password alone no longer opens the account. App-based codes defeat credential stuffing entirely; hardware security keys defeat phishing as well, because the key cryptographically verifies the real site."),
("p", "Priority order matters. Enable two-factor first on email, because email is the recovery key to everything else; then on banking, then on the accounts whose loss would hurt most. This single habit, more than any password policy, is what converts a breach from a catastrophe into an inconvenience."),
("h2", "Practical habits that do the work"),
("p", "Security advice fails when it demands constant effort. These habits are one-time decisions that keep paying:"),
("ul", [
"Generate passwords rather than inventing them, with sixteen characters or more as the default.",
"Store everything in a password manager and stop trying to remember individual passwords.",
"Use a four-word passphrase for the manager itself, long enough to be strong and rhythmic enough to remember.",
"Turn on two-factor authentication for email and banking first, then everything that offers it.",
"Never reuse a password across accounts, at any strength.",
"Check breach-notification services occasionally and change any password that appears in a leak.",
]),
("h2", "Common questions, answered honestly"),
("p", "Do passwords need changing every ninety days? Modern guidance says no: forced rotation encourages weak variations of the same password. Change on evidence, when a breach affects you, and otherwise invest the effort in length and uniqueness. Are password managers safe? Reputable ones are audited, encrypt data so the provider cannot read it, and are overwhelmingly safer than the alternatives, which are reuse and notebooks. Is writing passwords down wrong? A written password in a locked drawer is stronger than a reused password in your head; the risk model is physical, and physical security is something most people already do well."),
("p", "The deepest myth is that strong passwords are for people with something to hide. In reality, your accounts are infrastructure: email holds your recoveries, banking holds your money, and work accounts hold your livelihood. The attacker does not need to be interested in you specifically; automated systems try leaked credentials against millions of accounts indiscriminately, and weak passwords are how the machines pick their victims."),
("h2", "A fifteen-minute upgrade you can do today"),
("p", "The entire baseline takes one sitting. Install a password manager and set a strong master passphrase. Generate and replace the passwords on your email and banking accounts. Turn on two-factor authentication for both. That is fifteen minutes, and it moves you from the majority that can be breached by a leaked list to the minority whose accounts require an attacker to defeat a manager, a random password and a second factor at once. Few security upgrades offer a better return on fifteen minutes."),
("faq", [
("How long should a password be?", "Sixteen characters with mixed character sets is a strong default, and twenty or more for high-value accounts. Length contributes more to strength than any other single factor."),
("Are password managers really safe?", "Reputable managers encrypt your vault so only your master passphrase unlocks it, and most are independently audited. The realistic alternative, reusing memorable passwords, is dramatically less safe."),
("Is a passphrase better than a random password?", "Both work. A four-word random passphrase offers similar entropy to a random character password of moderate length and is easier to type; use passphrases where you must remember or speak the secret."),
("Do I need to change passwords regularly?", "No, not on a schedule. Change them when a breach affects the service, when you suspect malware, or when upgrading from a weak or reused password."),
("What about security questions?", "Treat their answers as passwords: random strings stored in your manager, not real facts, because the real facts about you are often publicly discoverable."),
("Is this browser password generator safe to use?", "Yes, when it generates locally with a cryptographic random source and never transmits the result, which is how this site's generator works. Close the tab after copying your password."),
]),
],
},
{
"slug": "json-explained",
"title": "JSON Explained: A Beginner's Guide to Reading and Formatting JSON",
"desc": "JSON is the data format behind nearly every modern API. A beginner-friendly guide to reading, writing, formatting and debugging JSON with confidence.",
"date": "2025-06-10",
"blocks": [
("p", "If you have ever looked at an API response, a configuration file, or a block of data shared between two programs, you have almost certainly seen JSON. It is the lingua franca of modern software: JavaScript objects on the surface, but read and written by every language, database and tool that matters. The name says JavaScript Object Notation, but its reach is far wider than JavaScript."),
("p", "For something so central, JSON is remarkably simple. This guide walks through what JSON actually is, how to read its syntax, the handful of mistakes that cause almost all errors, and the workflow for formatting and debugging it without pain."),
("h2", "What JSON is, in one paragraph"),
("p", "JSON is a text format for representing data as values: numbers, strings, booleans, null, and two containers called objects and arrays. An object maps keys to values inside curly braces; an array is an ordered list of values inside square brackets. Containers nest freely, so a value can be an object containing arrays of objects, which is how real-world data, a list of users, each with a list of orders, each with a list of items, is expressed naturally."),
("h2", "Reading the syntax"),
("p", "Objects are key-value pairs: the key is a string in double quotes, followed by a colon, followed by the value, with commas between pairs. Arrays are comma-separated values. Strings always use double quotes; numbers are written plainly; true, false and null are lowercase keywords. Whitespace between elements is insignificant, which is why the same data can be written beautifully indented or crushed into a single line."),
("p", "A small example makes the shape visible: an object with a name, a numeric age, and an array of hobbies, where each hobby is itself an object with a name and years. Reading JSON is mostly the skill of tracking which container you are inside, and indentation exists precisely to make that tracking visual rather than mental."),
("h2", "The five errors that cause almost every failure"),
("p", "When JSON fails to parse, and it will, the cause is almost always one of five things:"),
("ol", [
"A trailing comma after the last element in an object or array. JavaScript objects tolerate this; JSON does not.",
"Single quotes instead of double quotes. JSON requires double quotes for strings and keys.",
"Unquoted keys. In JavaScript, name is a valid key; in JSON it must be \"name\".",
"An unescaped line break or quote inside a string. Strings must stay on one line unless escaped.",
"Stray text after the final closing brace, often a second object pasted by accident.",
]),
("p", "Notice the pattern: four of the five are places where JSON is stricter than the JavaScript it descends from. That is the single most useful thing a beginner can internalise. JSON is a data interchange format, not a programming language, so it keeps only the plainest subset of syntax, and the errors you hit are almost always JavaScript habits leaking in."),
("h2", "Formatting: why pretty and compact both exist"),
("p", "The same data has two presentations, and both are correct. Formatted JSON, indented with each element on its own line, exists for humans: it makes nesting visible, and it is what you want when reading, reviewing or debugging. Minified JSON, all insignificant whitespace removed, exists for machines: it is smaller to store and faster to transmit, which is why APIs send it and why a raw API response looks like an endless line."),
("p", "The professional habit is to treat these as views of the same data. When a response arrives minified, format it to read it. When a file is ready for storage or transmission, minify it. Nothing about the data changes in either direction; only the whitespace does. A formatter that also validates, reporting the exact location of a syntax error, turns debugging from guessing into reading."),
("h2", "A debugging workflow that works"),
("p", "When something JSON-shaped is misbehaving, work in this order. First, format it. If formatting succeeds, the syntax is fine and the problem is in the data or the code handling it, which is a completely different and usually easier investigation. If formatting fails, read the error's position: JSON parsers name the line and character where they gave up, and the actual mistake is usually at or just before that spot."),
("p", "Second, check the five common errors from the list above, in order; the trailing comma alone accounts for a remarkable share of all failures. Third, verify the shape against what the consumer expects: a parseable object is not automatically the object the receiving code wanted, and a missing field or a null where a string was expected will fail downstream even though the JSON itself is valid. Finally, when sharing a problematic example, format it first; a readable snippet gets answered, an endless line gets ignored."),
("h2", "Why JSON won"),
("p", "It is worth knowing why this format displaced its predecessors. Compared with XML, JSON is lighter, needs no closing tags, maps directly onto the data structures of every major language, and is far easier to parse. Compared with binary formats, it is readable by humans and debuggable with nothing more than a text editor. Its only real cost is verbosity, and formats like protocol buffers exist for the cases where that cost matters. For everything else, and that is nearly everything, JSON's simplicity is exactly why it is everywhere."),
("p", "The practical takeaway is that JSON rewards a small investment in fluency. Learn the two containers, respect the stricter-than-JavaScript quoting rules, keep a formatter and validator one paste away, and the format stops being a source of friction and becomes what it was designed to be: a boring, reliable way for programs to share data."),
("faq", [
("What does JSON stand for?", "JavaScript Object Notation. It originated as a way to express JavaScript object literals as text, but it is now language-independent and used by virtually every programming ecosystem."),
("What is the difference between an object and an array?", "An object maps named keys to values inside curly braces; an array is an ordered list of values inside square brackets. Real data nests them freely."),
("Why does my JSON fail with single quotes?", "The JSON standard permits only double quotes for strings and keys. Single quotes are valid JavaScript but invalid JSON, and this is one of the most common beginner errors."),
("What is minified JSON for?", "Storage and transmission. Minifying removes insignificant whitespace, shrinking the payload without changing the data. Format it again whenever a human needs to read it."),
("How do I find where my JSON is broken?", "Use a validator that reports the error position. The parser names where it gave up, and the mistake is usually at or immediately before that location."),
("Is JSON only for JavaScript?", "No. Despite the name, JSON is a language-independent text format, and every mainstream language has mature libraries for reading and writing it."),
]),
],
},
]
